/**
	A datastream connected to the program's standard output.
	datastream@ cout;
	## Remarks:
		Standard output is usually the console window the program was started from, or a file or other program that the output has been redirected to.
		The stream is opened in text mode. Calling `close()` on it has no effect, since the program's standard output is not owned by the script.
		Unlike `println()`, writing to cout does not add a line break, so you have full control over the output. Because it is a datastream, cout can also be passed to any function that accepts one, such as `script_dump_engine_configuration()`.
*/

// Example:
void main() {
	cout.write("Loading");
	for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
		wait(100);
		cout.write(".");
	}
	cout.write(" done!\n");
}
